1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,105
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:09,005
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:13,740
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,676
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:24,085
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:27,663
On the day, the book is down 821 dollars.

7
00:00:27,663 --> 00:00:30,659
MetLife (MET) leads, +0.3 percent.

8
00:00:30,659 --> 00:00:32,951
Target (TGT) lags at -2.5.

9
00:00:32,951 --> 00:00:38,679
The checklist carries 2 items; details are in the written report.

10
00:00:38,679 --> 00:00:42,205
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

11
00:00:42,161 --> 00:00:51,039
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

12
00:00:51,039 --> 00:00:59,540
10 positions, led by MetLife, ticker MET, Airbnb, ticker ABNB, ACI Worldwide, ticker ACIW.

13
00:00:59,540 --> 00:01:12,385
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

14
00:01:12,347 --> 00:01:17,325
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

15
00:01:17,325 --> 00:01:19,814
Every morning OROS proposes.

16
00:01:19,814 --> 00:01:30,748
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

17
00:01:30,748 --> 00:01:34,838
Inside the book today: 2 positions up, 7 down.

18
00:01:34,838 --> 00:01:37,238
Best, MET at +0.32 percent.

19
00:01:37,238 --> 00:01:39,105
Worst, CCEP at -3.48.

20
00:01:39,105 --> 00:01:44,260
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

21
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:51,728
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -2.94% per trade over 1249 graded positions.

22
00:01:51,728 --> 00:01:59,017
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 50% position size.

23
00:01:59,017 --> 00:02:04,351
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

24
00:02:04,351 --> 00:02:16,974
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 0 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 50% until the record earns it back.

25
00:02:16,945 --> 00:02:19,204
Over to the sports desk.

26
00:02:19,204 --> 00:02:28,524
Day 89 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

27
00:02:28,524 --> 00:02:34,832
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 107,676.

28
00:02:34,832 --> 00:02:40,386
Then the index fund, in gray, 7.0 thousand back at 100,658.

29
00:02:40,386 --> 00:02:47,635
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 9.2 thousand back at 98,461.

30
00:02:47,635 --> 00:02:50,271
The race has no finish line.

31
00:02:50,271 --> 00:02:54,601
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

32
00:02:54,601 --> 00:02:59,496
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

33
00:02:59,496 --> 00:03:01,379
Nothing is restated.

34
00:03:01,379 --> 00:03:10,287
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

35
00:03:10,249 --> 00:03:17,584
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

36
00:03:17,584 --> 00:03:19,825
Nothing here is financial advice.

37
00:03:19,825 --> 00:03:22,814
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
